@*
    What a civic costs in content packs, and whether what you own suits.

    A tick or a cross rather than a colour, and for the same reason the conditions carry them: the
    game writes both directions and one of them is easy to read backwards. Corporate Dominion needs
    you to NOT have Megacorp - it is the civic for an oligarchy that cannot be one - and a plain
    badge saying "Megacorp" told a reader to go and buy the pack that takes it away.

    The mark says which way the gate runs; whether it is satisfied is said by the colour and by the
    tip. Those are two different facts and both are worth having: a pack you do not own is something
    you could buy, and a pack you own that rules a civic out is not a problem to solve at all.
*@

@foreach (var pack in Packs)
{
    <span class="sem-cell-chip sem-pack-chip @(pack.Satisfied ? null : "unmet")" title="@Told(pack)">
        <span class="mark" aria-hidden="true">@(pack.Wanted ? "✓" : "✗")</span>

        @if (pack.Icon is { Length: > 0 })
        {
            <GameIcon Path="@pack.Icon" Alt="" />
        }

        <span>@pack.Name</span>
    </span>
}

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The packs this civic depends on, in either direction.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required IReadOnlyList<CivicPack> Packs { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The whole of what one chip means, which is more than two words can carry.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Four sentences for four states, because every pair of them differs in something a reader
    /// would act on. Needing a pack you lack is a thing to buy; being ruled out by one you own is a
    /// thing to know and nothing to do about.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Told(CivicPack pack) => (pack.Wanted, pack.Held) switch
    {
        (true, true) => $"Needs {pack.Name}, which you have.",
        (true, false) => $"Needs {pack.Name}, which you do not have.",
        (false, false) => $"Only without {pack.Name}, which you do not have.",
        _ => $"Only without {pack.Name}, and you have it.",
    };
}
